          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            contains a higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            – that naturally couples to higher electrically charged 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"}> branes
            ,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membranes"} membranes
            (
            %a{:href => "#BergshoeffSezginTownsend87"}> Bergshoeff-Sezgin-Townsend 87
            ). By
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstrings"} superstrings
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#DuffHoweInamiStelle87"}> Duff-Howe-Inami-Stelle 87
            ). (See at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
          %p
            When in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten95"}> Witten95
            ) it was argued that the 10-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            theories of the five types of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} superstring theories
            are all limiting cases of one single 11-dimensional target space theory that extends
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
            ), it was natural to guess that this supergravity membrane accordingly yields a 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            that reduces in limiting cases to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
          %p
            But there were two aspects that make this idea a little subtle, even at this vague level: first, there is no good theory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the membrane
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            theories (which is fairly well understood).
          %p
            Later, with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} BFSS matrix model
            some people gained more confidence in the idea, by identifying the corresponding degrees of freedom in a special case (
            %a{:href => "#NicolaiHelling98"}> Nicolai-Helling 98
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DasguptaNicolaiPlefka02"} Dasgupta-Nicolai-Plefka 02
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane+matrix+model"} membrane matrix model
          %p
            In a more modern perspective, the M2-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            theory appears under
            %em
              %a{:href => "AdS-CFT#AdS4CFT3"} AdS4-CFT3 duality
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic dual
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} 4-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functional"} Green-Schwarz action functional
            is entirely controled by the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cocycle"} Lie algebra 4-cocycle
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+WZW+model"} higher dimensional WZW model
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            There are two different incarnations of the M2-brane. On the one hand it is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+sigma+model"} Green-Schwarz sigma model
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            that is a solution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            On the other hand the M2 also appears as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularity
            that looks from outside like a charged 2 dimensional object.
          %h3#as_a_greenschwarz_sigma_model As a Green-Schwarz sigma model
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+sigma+model"} Green-Schwarz sigma model
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
          %h3#AsABlackBrane As a black brane
          %h4#in_the_absence_of_4flux In the absence of 4-flux
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
            solution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field"} C-field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 8
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2,1} \times (\mathbb{R}^8-\{0\})
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            being
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 8
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g = H^{-2/3} g_{\mathbb{R}^{2,1}} + H^{1/3}g_{\mathbb{R}^8-\{0\}}
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mi β
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H = \alpha + \frac{\beta}{r^6}
          %p
            for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha,\beta) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \setminus \{(0,0)\}
          %p
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi vol
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F = d vol_{\mathbb{R}^{2,1}} \wedge \mathbf{d} H^{-1}
                  \\,.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mi β
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \beta \neq 0
            this is a 1/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+state"} BPS state
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+supergravity"} 11d supergravity
          %p
            In the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            the metric is ill-defined at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi α
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r = - (\beta/\alpha)^{1/6}
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#DuffGibbonsTownsend94"} Duff, Gibbons &amp; Townsend 1994, section 3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event+horizon"} event horizon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularity
            is at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r = 0
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near+horizon+geometry"} near horizon geometry
            of this spacetime is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freund-Rubin+compactification"} Freund-Rubin compactification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AdS
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-sphere"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
